my whole life i've been perfect little  meimei if you're not careful you might  just poof into a red panda from the year  it took to design panda may the hours  spent working from home and the creation  of boy band music the challenges were  endless making turning red the team  pulled it off though and happened to be  closer than ever by the end of it how  did they do it let's have a look  it's a little autobiographical this  story  time for a trip to the zoo  that is exactly what the team behind  turning red did the san francisco zoo  let a small group of the creative team  come to the zoo before it opened up to  the public for the day to let them get  up close and personal with some  real-life red pandas red pandas are  usually pretty lazy lucky for them it  was feeding time so the sketch artists  had the chance to draw out their anatomy  and how they moved around the red panda  was really challenging it was to build  in the computer animating it was tricky  but creating this panda based off of the  best source material there was was a  major help and they're such such cute  animals and i feel very fortunate to  have seen them um live this isn't like  any other panda you've seen panda may is  a very chunky panda making her  unbelievably cute even her friends think  so  having such unusual proportions meant  translating the way it moved into the 3d  world while retaining 2d qualities was a  huge hurdle anything naturally anything  with a lot of fur in the computer is  really hard to build and to make it look  just right the most challenging part  figuring out how to make those very  short legs get panda may around in a  natural enough looking way luckily it  was well worth the wait seeing the panda  version of may for the first time was a  special moment for everyone i'm a gross  red monster  [Applause]  if the tech doesn't exist why not invent  it a brand new technology was developed  and introduced for turning red to make  sure there was diversity throughout the  program would scan frames from the  different scenes to see what the  representation was like if the image  didn't meet the mark and really  reflecting the world we're living in  then the animating team would go back in  and make the adjustments needed  this diversifying included all the  details you could think of from small  ones like having a character that used  an insulin pump and including wheelchair  accessible ramps outside the temple it's  time to meet your new favorite boy band  the musical minds behind four town none  other than billy eilish and phineas  o'connell producer lindsay collins was  keen to get the two artists to sign on  to the movie so she went full fangirl  and made a scrapbook that mae would have  made full of four town pictures and  drawings including the faces of billy  and phineas on top collins wasn't really  sure they'd be into it but the duo was  open to a meeting with a creative team  and the deal was sealed we have three  songs in the in the movie that are  original four town songs the fact that  the musicians happen to be huge  animation fans helped phineas even lent  his sweet voice to the four town member  jessie boy bands it's the really catchy  melody and harmonies and claps and  choreography time for voice over magic  with all the recording happening during  the pandemic the performers were far  more isolated than usual sometimes they  were even halfway around the world pop  singer anne-marie recorded her cameo as  lauren from the uk with domici directing  her virtually that was so weird for me i  loved it though when the band had to get  together to nail those sweet sweet  harmonies they had to get a bit creative  with the booth you can't have a story  about a 13 year old girl without having  a boy band each performer had their own  hut built featuring windows all within  one studio so they could record on their  own but together it was all loads of fun  how many jokes did we miss  turning red is full of laugh-inducing  moments and while a lot of the jokes  wound up on the cutting room floor one  in particular came pretty close to  making the final cut it was when she's  like talking about they're talking about  the boys on the track and they're  sitting in the bleach oh yeah just how  corny was this joke well it speaks for  itself the joke was  are you a beaver cause damn  fingers crossed it'll end up in the  bonus features  caution the following images may induce  drooling how much do we want to eat all  the food we see on screen the secret to  making it look so appetizing was  somewhat surprising though food needs to  be oily  look oily and glossy in order for it to  feel delicious other drool inducing  details included making sure the colors  of the food were very vibrant and  saturated with just the right amount of  oily gloss  domici had a crystal clear vision from  the very beginning my biggest  cultural and artistic  inspirations and influences are like  disney and pixar and japanese anime the  liquid in water was styled in the same  way it is in anime with a kind of thick  and glue-like quality to it streaky  style light on windows was another nod  and of course the oh so expressive eyes  that allowed the animators to push the  character's emotion in a kind of  exaggerated way but perfectly in line  with the vibe of the movie 2d or 3d  or both  the animation in turning red is unlike  any other pixar movie we've seen and yet  it's still somehow familiar i think from  the beginning we really wanted to push  the style of this movie the team's goal  was a combination of the 2d and 3d world  not an easy ask with animators who've  been used to working and designing in a  certain way for a number of years did  ask the animators to go out of their  comfort zone supervising animator patty  kim was pretty nervous going into it  unsure the unique style domi had wanted  from the very beginning would actually  work out we were trying to figure out a  way to get the essence of 2d in a 3d  world eventually a balance was found and  used as a tool in the movie just when we  thought we couldn't love disney pixar  anymore working on a project this big  with a team virtually in the middle of a  pandemic can become stressful but the  vfx supervisor daniel feinberg wasn't  going to let that get the team down so  she created this thing called the power  panda poof uh weekly nominations every  single week different cast and crew  members would write kind things about  their co-workers spreading the love so  that during their crew tea time emails  could be read aloud for everyone to hear  could there be a more uplifting and  sweet event  everybody get cozy  i mean four years i've been working on  this film for four years and two of  those years involved working almost  exclusively remotely i mean we worked on  it for quite a while and for much of  that time we were in our homes turning  red was in pre-production for animation  when everything shut down a couple of  weeks later everyone was ready to go  full steam ahead set up to work from  home even if it wasn't always ideal we  made them the film from home  uh in our in our guest rooms on our  kitchen tables right what you know after  all  while domici never turned into a red  panda when she was growing up a whole  lot of other details in the movie were  drawn from her real life and the place  she grew up toronto so much of it you  can just trace it back to exactly like  her relationship with her mom and her  growing up even characters like each of  the girls in mae's close circle of  friends were based on a friend that she  had in her life at some point  at some points all the characters were a  bit uptight panda may took advantage of  that when she needed stacy to get out of  her way after working a certain way for  years the animators were reminded to  explore the options no no forget all of  that now let's try something totally  different don't move the body the team  took a chance on a special technology  called profile movers developed by bill  sheffler the tech could calculate  smaller movements and take care of  miniscule articulation points in a  character's bodies so the artists could  focus on the bigger picture and much to  their delight it worked like a charm  that was really cool welcome to turning  red aka the color palette of our dreams  that rona spent a lot of time thinking  about color on this film we've got may  and fire red and ming in the opposite  emerald green each of may's gal pals got  their own color based on their vibes as  well who runs the world girls  turning red marks the first pixar film  to have an all-female team in the  creative leadership roles  and it kind of happened by accident  there was a focus to put a team together  that would work well and this group of  women proved that they really did they  happened to work the most efficiently of  any pixar film in history only taking  about four years to create this movie  from scratch which is pretty short for  pixar  disney pixar has done it again  turning red brings the best of the  classic way of working to the newest  technologies which part of making the  movie do you think would have been most  challenging
 

